  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094795",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-42106",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row",
      "problem": "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot 6=-12.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
